<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kumaon Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1790 CE (approx.) · capital Champawat; later Almora</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Himalayan hill kingdom of the Chand rajas, who ruled Kumaon for centuries from Champawat and later Almora until the Gorkha conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to tradition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chand-dynasty">Chand dynasty</a> was founded by Som Chand, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> prince from the plains, who established himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champawat">Champawat</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a>. The early chronology is legendary, but by the 15th and 16th centuries the Chands had subdued rival hill chiefs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan-chand">Kalyan Chand</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a> around 1563.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rajas ruled a society of farming villages on terraced slopes, administered through local headmen and land grants to temples and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>. They warred with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/garhwal-kingdom">Garhwal Kingdom</a> and with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rohilla-state">Rohilla State</a> in the plains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baz-bahadur-chand">Baz Bahadur Chand</a>, a contemporary of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, extended the kingdom to the Tibetan border and is credited with building temples, including the Bhimeshwar temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhimtal">Bhimtal</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by court intrigue in the 18th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> was conquered by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha</a> army of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a>, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a> in 1790. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> of 1814 to 1816 the region passed to British control and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kumaon-kingdom.jpg" alt="Kumaon Kingdom" width="320" height="205"><figcaption>Image: Hyder Young Hearsey (1782-1840) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_and_the_capital_city_of_Kali_Kumaon,_Champawat,_1815.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1790 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Champawat; later Almora</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kalyan Chand, Baz Bahadur Chand</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kumaoni</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chand dynasty, Kingdom of Kumaon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1445&amp;focus=kumaon-kingdom" aria-label="Kumaon Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6767 1106 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kumaon Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7087 1362L7218 1408"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7218 1408h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7087 1362h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7087 1362h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Champawat. <a href="/atlas?year=1445&amp;focus=kumaon-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
